ng as well so it's a  novel heat exchange  technology um circuit is an electric  transportation company focused on  first and last mile zero emission had  subsidized  people really really love this service i  like to say that circuit is a a cash  flow positive  um ride hail company with operations  again in more than 20 cities here in the  us  and um you know with that i  i just want to make the call for um  founders and  and entrepreneurs and designers and  engineers who are tackling  some of these big challenges if you have  a solution and are looking for help  with customer development and traction  go to market  development and fundraising here in the  u.s  we would absolutely love to talk to you  i hope you all stay  safe i hope that you continue to be  excellent to each other  and enjoy the rest of tech innovation  thank you  good afternoon i'm delighted to be here  today to talk to you about how we can  prepare our cities  for pandemics but imagine this  imagine if we built our cities in a way  that had  built-in resilience for business and  life continuity  and that's what the idea of the  community block is all about  there's a wonderful quote by arundhati  roy nothing could be worse than a return  to normality  historically pandemics have forced  humans to break with the past  and imagine their world in you this one  is no different it's a portal or gateway  between one world and next and that is  my hope our hope  that after the year we've had what it  does for design  of our buildings of our neighborhoods of  our cities  will be so much better we knew  our cities had a problem when we started  seeing  worldwide of really foundational change  this is time square before and after the  pandemic  everything stopped and the fragility of  our cities  became so evident  we realized perhaps after a long time  that the health of humans the health of  systems  and our economic health was completely  interlinked  and so we started asking ourselves some  really fundamental questions  how can we keep cities sustainable  innovative and accessible  while creating resiliency in the face of  future pandemics  is it possible for us to survive the  next disease  outbreak without going through complete  lockdowns  can we conceive of a city block a  specific area  residents business owners even public  buildings that share a common purpose  which is built on a foundation of health  and wellness and is resilient  to pandemics in other words can we  maintain  business and life continuity through a  pandemic  by design and we really laser focused  on communities because we realized that  worldwide especially in the us  the approaches were either individual  focused  or really policy focused but the missing  rank  was a community and communities have the  power to enforce guidelines and support  individuals  not through laws but through social  bonds and physical connections  and that's what this idea of a prototype  community block is where  you really start with the premise that  the community  is the health system and you shift  dependency from self sufficiency  to really interdependency from rigidity  to flexibility  and most importantly from disparity to  equity  so what you'll see me share with you is  a range of strategies that start talking  about how we can achieve it  at its crux the concept of a community  block  is a community-based ecosystem that  supports  business and life continuity  it could be as a city block it could be  as a campus  it could be as a building the evidence  supporting it  is from urban studies on one end and  infection prevention on the other  and again the same idea that life  continuity  is business continuity and a resilient  solution works for human  environmental and physical health  there are six principles in the  community block  one it should be self-sustaining to  improve both  environmental health and social health  two  you have to have an integrated digital  infrastructure where  every footprint has a corresponding  cloud print so if you have to move  from physical space to digital space and  back again you can do that seamlessly  the idea of circuit and circuit breakers  which really is something singapore gave  to the rest of the world  allows the community to contain certain  sections  while continuing business and life in  others it's a powerful concept  the idea of flexible and mobile  infrastructure  we can no longer afford to have  infrastructure that is only  fixed but if you have flexibility and  mobility  you can have when needed rapid  adaptation  and decompression mixed-use  intergenerational and inclusive  approaches  can make so much of a difference so that  if one business takes a hit  the other one can really fill in  the intergenerational concept as well we  know how our  seniors and our aging adults were  overwhelmingly impacted  but if the strongest in the community  had been able to take care of them  then we wouldn't have to put all the  pressure on health care systems  and then finally all of this depends on  health  housing and education as the  cornerstones that support business and  life continuity  and to achieve these six principles  we have proposed 12 design strategies  and i really believe that these  strategies will keep evolving  many of you watching today will build  your own strategies  but they range anywhere from locally  produced food  to mixed-use multicultural community  development to urban park  networks rather than just having central  green spaces  intergenerational zoning like imagine if  senior living  and schools were co-located and adjacent  and could  really compensate for each other the  idea of thresholds we have seen how  important in between spaces have been  finding a way of leveraging that  connector spaces become wonderful buffer  spaces  as well um circuits and circuit breakers  being able to know what areas i can  quickly close off  having that positive building so that  you really are self-sustaining as a  community  the half-mile neighborhood a lot has  been written on that  if you can really reach things you need  without having to get on public  transport  it makes it so much more resilient cloud  print support we are no longer  able to separate our physical and  digital needs it has to be an integrated  approach  mobile infrastructure the idea of having  mobile carts  mobile clinics mobile libraries mobile  schools  that can be supporting the community  normally  but can quickly reconfigure during a  pandemic  ventilation and daylight has become so  important in this last year  and then thinking about mobility in  multiple scales  so if one scale of mobility becomes at  risk you have other scales that you can  rely on  right and the idea would be that if you  followed these strategies you would have  a  beautiful healthy community walkable  psychosocially supportive these mobile  pods could be distributed all over  as mobile clinics mobile art galleries  mobile libraries mobile schools  the idea of urban farms that produce  local food  really having visual and physical access  to nature  constantly so that you can improve  mental and physical health  these are all not new principles but  when you bring them together  you create a foundation of health and  wellness  and if you think about it in terms of  pandemic resilience  you think about just six levels that you  can pull  and imagine this that when you are  getting into a pandemic  hopefully we would not again but if we  do  or if we are needing it for the second  or the third wave  you have six levers that you can quickly  pull  that are activated by the community  these are checkpoints and access  circulation and flow making sure that  access to essential services is  maintained  during a normal mode people go to  essential services during pandemic board  essential services go to people and  that's where the mobility comes in  program shift being able to say okay  when hospitals need more space hotels  are going to be able to give it when  senior living  is compromised schools can be that  leftover space  understanding all of your community  assets  and understanding and planning for  resource sharing  so that no one is overburdened  de-densification and pedestrianization  and contact tracing so that you can  contain quickly  but imagine if contact racing is also  mobilized by the community  so you are not really fearing for  privacy as much because it's based on a  trust system  these are all really simple levers but  you pull them  and this wonderful block that you had  just  moves into pandemic mode and still  maintains life continuity so mixed-use  zoning for example  allows a hotel to flex from a hotel to a  quarantine facility  homes have thresholds so they can seek  the community they can visually connect  even though they're physically distant  there's limited car transportation but  you have pedestrianized streets to  distribute essential goals  you can start immediately protecting  your  compromised immuno-compromised  populations  and immediately create almost a wall of  defense around them  without compromising their social health  one of the toughest decisions we've had  to make in these times  is that to keep someone physically safe  we have had to compromise their  psychological safety  but if you build on a foundation of  health and wellness and you think about  what levers you can pull  you can maintain their social health as  well  so all of these strategies and  everything that i'm sharing with you is  open source  you can avail off the full document  um anytime that you need but this idea  of thinking of a very flexible system  that things to different scenarios but  because  it's built on health and wellness it can  flex  so easily for example what was an  urban farm and a grocery store can just  become a food delivery hub  and all these little mobile units that  were distributed across your community  now become centralized areas for tes